-- 0199 — the name was in the article title, not in the label.
--
-- ## What `0198` missed, and how
--
-- `0198` imported 723 concepts across five slices and dropped **90 of the 304
-- video games it fetched** without saying so. The list it lost:
--
--     Minecraft · Grand Theft Auto V · Tetris · Roblox · World of Warcraft
--     Fortnite · Counter-Strike · StarCraft · The Elder Scrolls V: Skyrim
--     PUBG: Battlegrounds · Grand Theft Auto: San Andreas, Vice City, III, IV
--
-- — which is to say, the games anybody would actually name. The vocabulary
-- gained 212 titles and not one of the fifteen most recognisable.
--
-- **The cause is a Wikidata convention rather than a bug in the data.** An
-- entity whose name is already the title of its English Wikipedia article
-- frequently carries **no English `label`**: Minecraft (Q49740) holds 43 labels
-- and 156 sitelinks, among them Korean `마인크래프트` and Chinese `我的世界`, and
-- no `en`. `SERVICE wikibase:label` answers such an entity with its own QID,
-- and `tools/wikidata_vocab.py` skipped anything so labelled — correctly, since
-- minting `Q49740` as somebody's interest would be worse than missing it, but
-- for the wrong reason: the name existed, in the place the tool was not looking.
--
-- **The repair is to read the article title too**, through
-- `schema:about`/`schema:isPartOf <https://en.wikipedia.org/>`, and use it where
-- the label is absent. That is a name the source states, in the language wanted,
-- so it is a fallback and not an invention. One trailing parenthetical is
-- stripped — an article is titled *"Cluedo (video game)"* where a label would
-- not be — and nothing more elaborate, because more would be guessing.
--
-- ## Why this is a second migration rather than a corrected first
--
-- `0198` is applied and published as ontology `0.31.0`, and a published version
-- is immutable. So this is the append that `0198` would have made: **the full
-- payload, re-fetched with the fallback in place**, of which the 723 keys
-- already held conflict away and 88 are new. Re-running the tool against the
-- same slices at the same bounds now yields this payload, so the tool and the
-- database agree again — which they did not for the hour between.
--
-- **Everything else is `0198`'s and unchanged**: the same five slices at the
-- same sitelink bounds, the same six authored merges, the same refusals, the
-- same `review_required` policy, the same five-table copy-forward, and the same
-- two rules that caught real defects — every concept must reach a hub, and no
-- refused topic may become resolvable.
--
-- **What it does not repair.** The other four slices are affected by the same
-- convention and gained nothing here, which is itself the measurement: sports,
-- crafts, art movements and academic disciplines are common nouns, and a common
-- noun has an English label. Only titled works — games, films, albums — hide
-- their names in the article. Any future slice of *works* must ask for both.

begin;

create temporary table wikidata_payload (data jsonb) on commit drop;
